                <pb facs="0233" n="135" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg135"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 King, Clifford J./8: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-06-16T12:00:00+08:00">June 16,
                        1930—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received June 16—9:10 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>49. The following telegram has been sent to the Legation: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 16, noon. Reverend Clifford King has been released from custody of
                            bandits and is unharmed. The local garrison at Loshan effected the
                            rescue by sending about 160 soldiers to the village where ascertained
                            King was being kept by the bandits and forcibly brought him to safety.
                            Repeated to Nanking and Department.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
